mac: hand a key the application did not take to the next responder.

keyDown: and keyUp: dispatched the event and returned, so the key stopped at the
view no matter what the application made of it. That is wrong for any embedded
view, which is somebody else's first responder: the host above it never sees a key
it should have had. The visible cost in a plugin is that the host's key commands
and its on-screen keyboard go dead while the plugin window has the focus.

PUGL_FAILURE from puglDispatchEvent is the application saying it did not want the
key, and [super keyDown:] is Cocoa's way of passing one on. An application that
returns PUGL_SUCCESS - which is every application that returns nothing in
particular, since PUGL_SUCCESS is zero - is unaffected.

**The status answers "is this keystroke mine", not "did this handler act on it".**
A view with a text field in it must claim a printable key even though the work is
done by the PUGL_TEXT event rather than by the key event, because the unhandled
path hands the key on *instead of* calling interpretKeyEvents: - a key the
application refused is not text it is composing, and the responder it goes to will
do its own interpreting. Claim it and both still happen, as before. This is the one
behaviour change for an application that already distinguishes its return values.
diff --git a/src/mac.m b/src/mac.m
index 977cd7e..d8dafac 100644
--- a/src/mac.m
+++ b/src/mac.m
@@ -692,7 +692,15 @@ handleCrossing(PuglWrapperView* view, NSEvent* event, const PuglEventType type)
 
   PuglEvent pressEvent;
   pressEvent.key = ev;
-  puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &pressEvent);
+  const PuglStatus st = puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &pressEvent);
+
+  // PUGL_FAILURE means the application did not take the key. Hand it on, or it dies here:
+  // an embedded view is the host's first responder while its window has the focus, so
+  // swallowing every key costs the host its own shortcuts and its virtual keyboard.
+  if (st == PUGL_FAILURE) {
+    [super keyDown:event];
+    return;
+  }
 
   if (!spec) {
     [self interpretKeyEvents:@[event]];
@@ -723,7 +731,9 @@ handleCrossing(PuglWrapperView* view, NSEvent* event, const PuglEventType type)
 
   PuglEvent releaseEvent;
   releaseEvent.key = ev;
-  puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &releaseEvent);
+  if (puglDispatchEvent(puglview, &releaseEvent) == PUGL_FAILURE) {
+    [super keyUp:event];  // as in keyDown:, and in pairs: a host that saw the press wants it
+  }
 }
 
 - (BOOL)hasMarkedText
